# --- line endings ------------------------------------------------------------
# Source is stored AND checked out as LF everywhere: kills the perpetual
# "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warnings and makes checkouts identical on
# Windows and the Linux CI runner.
*.py   text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.csv  text eol=lf

# LightGBM text-format model dumps are parsed by an offset-based reader that
# assumes LF line endings; autocrlf=true checkouts on Windows corrupt them to
# CRLF, which crashes lgb.Booster.predict() at runtime ("Model format error,
# expect a tree here"). Force git to leave these bytes untouched everywhere.
models/**/*.txt -text

# --- binaries ----------------------------------------------------------------
*.duckdb  binary
*.parquet binary
*.gz      binary
*.pkl     binary

# --- merge rules -------------------------------------------------------------
# The DuckDB file mutates on both CI and locally and git cannot merge a
# binary. It used to carry `merge=ours`, which resolved that SILENTLY by
# keeping one side, on the assumption that the idempotent gap-fill would
# re-ingest whatever was discarded.
#
# That holds for prices and events. It does not hold for state no amount of
# re-scraping rebuilds — above all model_registry, which records which model
# is live. On 2026-08-23 a rebase discarded a freshly promoted model and the
# system quietly went back to one trained through 2024-11-21. No error, no
# failed build, nothing to notice.
#
# The rule is gone: a concurrent change now CONFLICTS, loudly, and a human
# decides. The state that cannot be re-derived is additionally mirrored to
# sorted JSON in data/state/, which merges as text and is restored on every
# run (vectora/state.py), so the binary is a cache rather than the only copy.
data/vectora.duckdb -merge
